    "innovation": "Using an Earth system model, this paper reveals a major east-west migration of deep water masses in the South Atlantic during the last interglacial, demonstrating that large regional circulation changes can occur even when the overall Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation strength remains stable.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Atlantic Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Understanding how the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) evolved during crucial past geological periods is important in order to decipher the interplay between ocean dynamics and global climate change. Previous research, based on geological proxies, has provided invaluable insights into past AMOC changes. However, the causes of the changes in water mass distributions in the Atlantic during different periods remain mostly elusive.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "South Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "For instance, several studies, which include sediment records from the South Atlantic (SA), suggest that the overturning circulation reversed during some critical geological periods.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "NorESM",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "equilibrium",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "Here, we examine the spatial structure and variations of AMOC at two time periods during the last interglacial (115 ka and 125 ka) through analyzing quasi-equilibrium simulations of a state-of-the-art Norwegian Earth System Model20 (NorESM, see method for details).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Northern Component Water",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "South Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "This simulated feature is consistent with the \u03b5Nd records from the NA22, but appears to contrast with the interpretation of sediment proxy data from the SA, which suggests increased NCW in the SA at ~115 ka13.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Deep Western Boundary Current",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "In the NA, a shoaling of the Deep Western Boundary Current (DWBC) during the last interglacial has been suggested in response to a reduced presence of Lower NADW (LNADW)31.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Deep Water",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "South Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "However, shoaled NADW at 115 ka results in a stronger control of the NCW at intermediate depths (e.g., between 2000 m and 3000 m) in the SA (Fig. 6a,d) but allows SCW to influence a larger volume in the deep SA basins below 4000 m (Fig. 6e,f).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
